# TWC Mission Server Cline Rules

## Purpose
Workspace-level rules for Cline in VS Code. This file is the Cline equivalent of Continue's `.continue/rules/*.md` and is auto-injected into every Cline prompt in this workspace (no `globs:` scoping available). Keep it markdown-only and concise.

## Conventions
- Line endings: CRLF only (verify with `git ls-files --eol`; never introduce mixed endings).
- C# / .NET Framework targeting the IL-2 CloD engine (Team Fusion 4.312+ / Desert Wings Tobruk / Blitz).
- Scripts require `#define DEBUG` / `#define TRACE` and `//$reference parts/core/gamePlay.dll` (etc.) at the top.
- Credit TWC - The Wrecking Crew (http://twcclan.com/) for reuse/snippets.

## Overall Project layout - several missions and independent modules in different directories
Only the files within ./Genghis are actively being developed in now and should be actively consulted for relevant information and possible cross-module interaction, and actively edited.  The rest are older campaigns and missions that are no longer being actively developed - of possible interest for history or specific techniques, but most everything of interest is already in ./Genghis.

 - ./Genghis - our main campaign and project for now.  This is the only directory we are actively developing in now.  On the English Channel map.
 - ./Campaign21 - an older campaign mission variant, On the English Channel map.
 - ./M001-M003 - yet older campaign mission variants, On the English Channel map.
 - ./Testing - a yet older campaign mission call Escort21, On the English Channel map.
 - ./Tobruk_Campaign - the most recent campaign mission variant other than Genghis, on the Tobruk map and never completely finished.
 - ./autohotkey-autolauncher-start-tools - a set of scripts to automate the launching of the server and the campaigns on the server.
 - ./CloDMissionCommunicator - the inter-module communication layer for the server and the campaigns, legacy but still used here and there in the code.
 - ./radar - .html and .js files for the radar display, hosted on a separate web server; the campaign server uploads various files to this server and the ./js files interpret and display them as a map plus list of information about the current campaign status, objectives, reconnaissance results, etc
 - ./res some images and icons used in the campaign, particularly the radar display

